<Base title="About" description="Noam Borgnia is a master’s student at the Technical University of Munich working in probability and mathematical statistical mechanics, with projects on cluster aggregation and the Ising model.">
  <header class="page-head">
    <h1>About</h1>
  </header>
  <div class="bio">
    <p>
      I&rsquo;m Noam Borgnia, a master&rsquo;s student at the Technical University of Munich.
      I studied mathematics at Princeton University, graduating in 2024. My interests are
      in probability and mathematical statistical mechanics: how randomness and local
      interactions shape the behavior of large systems.
    </p>
    <p>
      My master&rsquo;s thesis, supervised by Noam Berger, studies
      <a href="/research/cluster-cluster-aggregation/">cluster&ndash;cluster aggregation</a>.
      I&rsquo;m interested in how clusters grow as they move and merge, the geometry they
      leave behind, and what changes when the dynamics conserve momentum.
    </p>
    <p>
      My <a href="https://dataspace.princeton.edu/handle/88435/dsp01dv13zx56v">bachelor&rsquo;s thesis</a>,
      supervised by Jacob Shapiro, asked how the complex zeros of the Ising partition
      function relate to the decay of spin correlations. That question grew into
      <a href="/research/lee-yang-gap/">ongoing work with Jacob and Jui-Hui Chung on the Lee&ndash;Yang gap and mass gap</a>.
      I&rsquo;m also interested in understanding Lee&ndash;Yang zeros in the random-field Ising model.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site brings together my research, computational experiments, expository
      writing, and teaching materials.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
